messages:
Mira: Quick question — I'm updating TF for the gpu nodepool to prefer spot but fall back to a capacity reservation. Two things: (1) how should we wire the instance profile / IAM role so the nodes still have KMS/SSM access when they come up from CR? (2) what spot interruption/grace settings do we use so pods get a fair pre-drain? PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/4782

Jaspar: @Mira if CR = capacity reservation (on-demand reserve) then instance profile is the same as normal. The trick is ensuring the launch template references the capacity_reservation_specification for AWS. Are we deploying in a single AZ or multi-AZ?

Devon: yes AWS. here's a trimmed TF snippet I used before:
```hcl
resource "aws_launch_template" "gpu" {
  name_prefix = "gpu-spot-"
  iam_instance_profile { name = aws_iam_instance_profile.gpu.name }
  instance_market_options {
    market_type = "spot"
    spot_options {
      instance_interruption_behavior = "terminate"
      max_price = ""
    }
  }
  capacity_reservation_specification {
    capacity_reservation_preference = "open"
  }
}
```

Devon: For fallback, we create an `aws_capacity_reservation` in the desired AZs and ensure the autoscaler/nodepool config prefers those AZs. The instance profile stays the same — just make sure the role has `kms:Decrypt` and `ssm:GetParameter`.

Akeem: For graceful eviction we run the node-termination-handler/spot-interrupt daemonset. Handler should sleep ~120s to allow pods to receive SIGTERM and pre-drain. Our typical flow: handler receives EC2 interruption -> cordon node -> wait 120s -> `kubectl drain --ignore-daemonsets --delete-local-data --force` -> terminate.

Jaspar: Also add kubelet flags to reduce noisy evictions: `--eviction-hard=memory.available<100Mi,nodefs.available<10%`. And taint spot nodes with `node.kubernetes.io/spot=true:NoSchedule` + tolerations on GPU workloads (so only intended pods land there).

infra-bot: Deploy check: infra/ci/launch-template-lint passed ✅ https://ci.redwood/internal/12345

Mira: sweet. For SSM/KMS permissions, do we add them to the existing `redwood-node-role` or make a separate managed policy?

Devon: add to the node-role (managed policy attached in TF) to keep instance boot flow simple. Keep scope narrow: `kms:Decrypt` on the specific key ARN and `ssm:GetParameter` for path `/redwood/*`. Example TF snippet: 
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "node_kms_ssm" {
  name = "node-kms-ssm"
  role = aws_iam_role.redwood_node.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Action = ["kms:Decrypt"], Resource = "arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123456789012:key/abcd-ef01", Effect = "Allow" },
      { Action = ["ssm:GetParameter"], Resource = "arn:aws:ssm:us-east-1:123456789012:parameter/redwood/*", Effect = "Allow" }
    ]
  })
}
```

Akeem: FYI if you use capacity reservations across AZs, CRs are zonal. Make sure the autoscaler has AZ affinity or you might end up booting spot instances where no CR exists (and lose the fallback benefit).

Mira: thanks all — I'll update the PR to include the launch_template snippet, the node-role policy, and the 120s interrupt hook. Will ping when ready for review. :thumbsup:
